            <ArticleTitle>Hypertension-associated hypalgesia: a clue to the comorbidity of headache and other pain disorders.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>46-50</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1600-0404.2009.01215.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Primary headaches and chronic musculoskeletal pain are prevalent disorders with incompletely known causes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To review the data from the HUNT studies on the comorbidity of these pain disorders, and their relation to blood pressure levels.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS &amp; METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Cross-sectional and prospective data from more than 50,000 persons participating in the HUNT studies, a large Norwegian population-based health survey</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Headache and chronic musculoskeletal complaints in all parts of the body were comorbid, and the prevalence of pain in all locations was inversely related to blood pressure levels.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A likely cause for this is hypertension-associated hypalgesia, described in both animal and human experimental models, involving interactions between cardiovascular and pain modulating centres at the brainstem level, and probably also peripheral baroreceptor mechanisms.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Better understanding of these mechanisms may be crucial for enabling better prevention and treatment of these very prevalent, costly and disabling disorders.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
